Welcome to notebook seven. The previous notebook gave you classes, objects, and traits, which is the imperative and object oriented shape of Scala. This notebook gives you the form that most real Scala code actually reaches for. Case classes for immutable data records, and enums for finite sets of alternatives. Together they are Scala's expression of algebraic data types, which is the backbone of domain modeling in the language.

Two ideas to anchor on before we dive in. A case class is a product type. A record that bundles several fields together. You can read it as, this and this and this. A user has a name and an email and an age. An enum is a sum type. A choice between a fixed set of alternatives. You read it as, this or this or this. A shape is a circle or a rectangle or a triangle. Most real world domains are built by combining the two. Products containing sums containing products. And pattern matching, in the next notebook, is how you take them apart again to write logic over them.

Let us start with the case class. The single most idiomatic data shape in Scala. You prefix the class keyword with the case keyword and Scala 3 does a remarkable amount of work for you. Take, case class User, with three parameters, name as a String, email as a String, age as an Int. That one line gives you, for free, the following. First, every constructor parameter becomes a public val field automatically. You do not need to write val in front of each parameter. Second, a companion object with an apply method is generated, so you call User of arguments without writing the new keyword. Third, a companion unapply method is generated, which is what powers pattern matching on the type, as we will see in notebook eight. Fourth, a sensible toString method that prints something like User open paren ganesh comma g at example dot com comma thirty five close paren, instead of an opaque memory address. Fifth, structural equals and hashCode. Two instances with the same field values compare equal. Sixth, a copy method, which we will look at in a moment. And seventh, automatic serialization. The compiler marks case classes as Serializable, which becomes important in Spark because closures and data shipped between executors must be serializable.

That whole list is the difference between a case class and a plain class. You almost never write a plain class to hold data in Scala. You write a case class. Reach for plain class only when you genuinely need encapsulated mutable state, like the bank account example in the previous notebook.

The copy method deserves its own section. Case classes are immutable. So how do you produce a slightly different version of an existing instance? You call copy. The copy method takes the same parameters as the primary constructor, but each one has a default that is the existing value from this instance. So you only mention the fields you want to change. The rest stay the same.

For example, if u is a User named ganesh aged thirty five, calling u dot copy with age equals thirty six gives you a new User with the same name and email but age thirty six. The original u is unchanged. Calling u dot copy with name and email both supplied gives you a new User with those new values and the same age. The pattern is, the same record, with these fields different. Copy is how you express state change in idiomatic Scala. Produce a new value. Do not mutate the old one. This pattern matters because immutable values can be shared safely. Mutated values cannot. Notebook nine will lean on copy heavily when modelling error handling flows.

Now equality. This is one of the most important distinctions between case classes and plain classes. Plain classes use reference equality. Two instances are equal only if they point to the same object in memory. Case classes use structural equality. Two instances are equal if all their fields are equal.

Two operators give you the two kinds of equality. The double equals operator is structural equality. It calls the equals method, which case classes override to compare fields. This is the opposite of java, where the double equals on objects is reference equality. The eq operator, lowercase e q, is reference equality in Scala. It is rarely needed. Reach for it only when you specifically care whether two names point to the same object.

Structural equality also gives you a working hashCode. The compiler derives hashCode from the same fields used in equals, so equal instances have equal hash codes. Which means case classes just work as keys in a Map, as elements in a Set, and as identifiers when shipped across distributed systems.

Case class parameters can have default values, and callers can pass arguments by name. The same rules apply as for method parameters from notebook three. Take a Server case class with three parameters. Host, port with default eight thousand eighty, and useTls with default false. You can construct a Server with just the host, in which case port and useTls take their defaults. You can construct one positionally with all three values. Or you can construct one by name, like Server of host equals the string prod example dot com, port equals four hundred forty three, useTls equals true. Named arguments at the call site read like prose. They are unambiguous in a way that positional arguments are not, especially when several parameters share the same type.

There is one variant worth meeting briefly. The case object. When a case has no fields, when it is essentially a singleton that participates in pattern matching and structural equality the same way case classes do, use case object instead of case class. You write, case object Empty, and that is it. Empty is a single, named instance. It has its own toString that just prints the name. It can be matched in patterns. You will see case object most often as one of the alternatives of a sum type, the cases that carry no data. With Scala 3's enum, you usually do not have to write case object by hand anymore. The enum form handles both shapes uniformly.

Now we move from products to sums. The enum keyword. Scala 3 introduced a proper enum keyword. The simplest use is a closed set of named values. What java's enum does. You write, enum Color, then on the next line, indented, case Red comma Green comma Blue. That is it. Color is a type. Red, Green, and Blue are three values of that type. The compiler generates three useful members for free. Ordinal gives you the zero based position of a case, so Red dot ordinal is zero, Green is one, Blue is two. Color dot values gives you an array of every case in declaration order. And Color dot valueOf gives you a lookup by name string, throwing if the name does not match a case.

But Scala 3 enums go further than java's enums. Each case can carry its own fields and types. When cases carry data, the enum becomes a true sum type. A value is one of several alternatives, and each alternative has its own shape.

The canonical example is shapes. An enum called Shape with three cases. Circle, which carries a radius of type Double. Rectangle, which carries width and height. Triangle, which carries base and height. Each case is essentially a case class. Shape dot Circle of two point zero is a value of type Shape, specifically of subtype Shape dot Circle, with one field called radius. It has structural equality, a sensible toString, a copy method, and pattern matching support. The same package as a regular case class.

What you have just declared is the canonical algebraic data type. A Shape is a Circle or a Rectangle or a Triangle. And each option carries its own data. This compositional shape is what notebook eight's pattern matching exists to consume.

An enum can freely mix cases that carry data and cases that do not. The classic example is an option shaped type. You declare, enum Maybe of a type parameter A, then case Some of value of type A, then case None. Some carries a value. None does not. Scala compiles the no data case to a singleton, the same as a case object, and the data case to a case class like subtype. The whole enum represents zero or one value of type A.

There is a small annotation in that example, the plus sign in front of the type parameter A. That is a variance annotation. We will explain variance properly in notebook ten. For now, read the plus A as, Maybe is covariant in A, which means a Maybe of Int can be used where a Maybe of Any is expected. The standard library's Option of A, which you have been meeting since notebook four when we discussed Map dot get, is structurally exactly this same shape.

An enum can also declare methods that all cases inherit, just like a trait. Cases can also override them. Take a Shape enum with two cases, Circle and Rectangle, and define an area method directly inside the enum. The area method uses pattern matching to decide which formula to apply. If the shape is a Circle with radius r, the area is pi times r times r. If the shape is a Rectangle with width w and height h, the area is w times h. Then any shape value has an area method available on it. Shape dot Circle of two point zero dot area gives you about twelve point five seven. Shape dot Rectangle of three and four dot area gives you twelve.

Two new ideas appear in that example. The this match expression, which starts a pattern match against the current value. Notebook eight unpacks pattern matching fully. And methods on the enum itself. The method lives on every case. That is how you put behaviour next to the data shape it operates on, while keeping the data shape closed. No surprise new cases can be added by a downstream library, because the compiler knows the full list of alternatives the moment you finish writing the enum.

Most real domain types are products inside sums. A trading event for example might be a Trade with a symbol, a price, and a quantity. Or a Quote with a symbol, a bid price, and an ask price. Or a Heartbeat with a timestamp. That outer choice between Trade, Quote, and Heartbeat is a sum. The fields inside each case are a product. Together they form a complete algebraic data type. You express that in Scala as an enum with three parameterised cases. Four lines of code gives you the entire data shape.

Three things you get automatically by writing those four lines. First, the compiler knows the full list of alternatives. Pattern matches that miss a case will trigger an exhaustiveness warning. Second, each alternative has named fields, structural equality, a copy method, and a clean toString. Third, adding a new alternative, say a Cancel case with an order identifier field, is a single line of code, and the compiler tells you every place in your codebase that needs to handle the new alternative. That last point is huge in practice. It turns the type system into a worklist. Add a case. The compiler enumerates the broken sites for you. This is what type safe domain modelling means in real engineering.

A small decision guide before we close. When do you reach for what? For a record with several fields and no alternatives, use case class. For a finite set of named tags with no data, use enum with no argument cases. For a choice between several shapes, each carrying its own data, use enum with parameterised cases. For behaviour mixable into many unrelated types, use trait. For per instance mutable state, use plain class.

Let us close with a small but realistic model. A payment system that knows three payment methods, and produces an outcome that is either a success or one of two failure modes. We model PaymentMethod as an enum with three parameterised cases. Card, with a card number and an expiry. BankTransfer, with an international bank account number. And Wallet, with a provider name and an account identifier. We model PaymentResult as another enum with three cases. Success, with a transaction identifier and an amount. Declined, with a reason string. And Failed, with an error string. Then we wrap a single Payment as a case class with an amount, a method, and a result.

Notice the composition. Payment is a product. One record with three fields. PaymentMethod and PaymentResult are sums. Each is one of several alternatives. One of Payment's fields is itself a sum. Products containing sums containing products is the normal shape of domain data in any non trivial application. And the copy method lets you derive a refunded version from a successful payment without touching the original payment value.

Walking the result field of a payment to decide what to do with it, log the success, alert on the failure, retry the decline, is exactly the job pattern matching was designed for. Notebook eight introduces match expressions and shows how the compiler enforces exhaustiveness. Write a match against PaymentResult, miss the Declined case, and the compiler tells you with a warning. The match keyword is the partner to every enum you wrote in this notebook.
